==Old English==

===Etymology===
From earlier {{term|æces|lang=ang}} (cf. also Northumbrian {{term|acas|lang=ang}}), from {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|akwisī|lang=gem-pro}}, probably from a {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|h₂egʷs-ih₂-||axe|lang=ine-pro}}, from {{recons|h₂eḱ-||sharp, pointed|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|axa|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|akus|lang=osx}} (Low German {{term|Ax|lang=nds}}, {{term|Äx|lang=nds}}, {{term|Ex|lang=nds}}), Dutch {{term|aaks|lang=nl}}, Old High German {{term|ackus|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Axt|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|øx|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|økse|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|yxa|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌶𐌹|tr=aqizi|lang=got|sc=Goth}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/æks/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|g=}}

# an [[axe]], used as a weapon or cutting-tool

